const QUESTIONS = [{"n":1,"topic":2,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image1\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou need to implement agent2 to meet the technical requirements.<br />\nHow should you complete the YAML configuration? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image2\" src=\"images/image2.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image3.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A custom agent (a <b>.agent.md</b> file) is a saved persona for Copilot: its <b>tools</b> list decides what it may do. <b>search</b> finds files and code, <b>read</b> opens and inspects them, <b>edit</b> changes files, <b>execute</b> runs shell commands, <b>agent</b> delegates to subagents, <b>web</b> browses the internet. A planning-only agent must look but never touch.</div><div class=\"why\">Box 1 = <b>search</b>, Box 2 = <b>read</b>: everything a plan needs, nothing it must not do.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>edit</b> would let the planner rewrite files.</li><li><b>execute</b> would let it run local commands.</li><li><b>agent</b> delegation is unnecessary for writing a plan.</li><li><b>web</b> is redundant: Microsoft Learn access already comes from the listed microsoftdocs MCP tools.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (tools, handoffs)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":2,"topic":2,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image4\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nBefore App1 is upgraded, you need to verify each individual upgrade step and whether all tests have passed.<br />\nWhich file should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tassessment.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tplan.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tagent.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ttasks.md<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The Copilot app-modernization agent upgrades legacy apps in three stages. <b>assessment.md</b> is the analysis report (breaking changes), <b>plan.md</b> is the strategy, and <b>tasks.md</b> is the live dashboard where every step flips through start, execute, validate (build + test), complete.</div><div class=\"why\">To verify each step and test results, read the live dashboard: <b>tasks.md</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A assessment.md</b> is written before the upgrade: analysis only, no step status.</li><li><b>B plan.md</b> describes strategy; it never tracks execution.</li><li><b>C agent.md</b> is not a modernization artifact at all.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-app-modernization/concepts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App modernization: assessment, plan, tasks.md</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-upgrade/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot upgrade: how the stages work</a></li></ul>"},{"n":3,"topic":2,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that has a GitHub Actions workflow. The workflow runs an AI agent.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the default GITHUB_TOKEN permissions are read-only, and write access is granted to only the job that performs repository write operations. The workflow must be able to create and approve pull requests only when explicitly enabled.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image5\" src=\"images/image4.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image5.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Every Actions workflow gets a <b>GITHUB_TOKEN</b> proving its identity. <b>permissions</b> blocks decide what that token may touch. <b>contents</b> covers repo files, <b>pull-requests</b> covers PR operations, <b>issues</b> covers issues. Least privilege = default read-only, grant write only where needed.</div><div class=\"why\">Top-level <b>contents: read</b> (read-only default) · job <b>contents: write</b> (only the writer job) · step <b>pull-requests: write</b> (explicit PR create/approve).</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>permissions: read-all</b> is not the required default here, and read-only never grants write.</li><li><b>issues: write</b>: nothing in the workflow touches issues.</li><li><b>pull-requests: read</b> cannot create or approve PRs.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-guides/automatic-token-authentication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GITHUB_TOKEN permissions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":4,"topic":2,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues and create draft pull requests. The repository uses GitHub Actions for CI, and reviewers rely on pull request timelines and workflow artifacts to understand what the agent did.<br />\nDuring long-running agent tasks, the reviewers lose track of decisions and validation steps, which causes repeated questions and reworks when context drifts between iterations.<br />\nYou need to persist task progress and decisions as durable artifacts and ensure that the reviewers can verify what the agent did during and after execution by using GitHub as the system of record.<br />\nWhat should you do for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image7\" src=\"images/image6.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image7.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Reviewers cannot see inside an agent run, so GitHub is the system of record: <b>artifacts</b> (upload-artifact) persist files after a run, the <b>PR body + timeline</b> narrate progress on the PR itself, and <b>View session</b> streams the live log.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>upload-artifact + retention</b> = durable downloadable outputs · <b>PR body/timeline</b> = reviewable signals · <b>View session</b> = real time.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Assigning an issue starts work but records nothing.</li><li>Approve-and-run is a manual gate, not tracking.</li><li>Mentioning @copilot on a merged PR restarts the agent instead of tracking it.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/storing-workflow-data-as-artifacts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow artifacts</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":5,"topic":2,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues asynchronously.<br />\n<br />\nWhen an issue is assigned to GitHub Copilot, the agent creates a draft pull request, but your team cannot always tell whether the agent is actively working, has completed its session, or is awaiting workflow approval.<br />\nWhich execution context does each signal indicate? To answer, drag the appropriate context to the correct signals. Each signal may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image9\" src=\"images/image8.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image9.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> When you assign an issue to Copilot, it reacts and works: an <b>eyes reaction</b> means it accepted, <b>timeline updates</b> narrate the run, and if <b>Actions checks sit idle</b>, the repo likely requires manual workflow approval (common for agent PRs).</div><div class=\"why\">Eyes = <b>acknowledged, PR coming</b> · timeline started = <b>actively running</b> · checks idle = <b>human must approve and run workflows</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":6,"topic":2,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent and supports hooks stored under .github/hooks.<br />\nYou need a Shell command to run automatically whenever an agent execution fails.<br />\nWhich type of hook should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tpostToolUse<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tsessionEnd<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\terrorOccurred<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tagentStop<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The Copilot CLI supports <b>hooks</b>: small shell scripts fired on lifecycle events. <b>postToolUse</b> runs after each tool call, <b>sessionEnd</b> at session end, <b>errorOccurred</b> when an execution fails.</div><div class=\"why\">A command on every agent failure needs the failure event: <b>errorOccurred</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>postToolUse</b> fires after every tool call, failures or not.</li><li><b>sessionEnd</b> fires once at the end regardless of outcome.</li><li><b>agentStop</b> is about the agent stopping, not about an execution error.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":7,"topic":2,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You are about to start a complex refactoring task in the GitHub Copilot CLI.<br />\nBefore Copilot makes any changes, you need to review and agree on the approach.<br />\nWhat should you do first?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the Copilot CLI and specify the --agent=Task parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the Copilot CLI and specify the --allow-all parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tFrom the Copilot CLI, switch to plan mode.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tFrom the Copilot CLI, run the /compact command.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Plan mode</b> is a CLI working style: Copilot investigates, writes up its proposed approach, and waits for your approval before changing a single file. Flags like <b>--allow-all</b> do the opposite (skip all approvals); <b>/compact</b> only shrinks conversation context.</div><div class=\"why\">Review-then-agree before any change = <b>plan mode</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A --agent=Task</b> only picks an agent; it adds no approval gate.</li><li><b>B --allow-all</b> removes approvals entirely.</li><li><b>D /compact</b> manages context size; it produces no approvable plan.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":8,"topic":2,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  A team assigns an issue to the GitHub Copilot coding agent by using the following one-line description: Fix the login bug.<br />\nCopilot creates a pull request, but the pull request is missing changes and has an incorrect scope.<br />\nHow should you resolve the issue?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable Copilot memory.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate the Model Context Protocol (MCP) server rate limits.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd a clear description of the problem to the issue.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAllocate more resources to copilot-setup-steps.yml.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The coding agent plans from the issue text: a one-liner with no scope, no expected changes and no acceptance criteria leaves it guessing, so the PR comes back thin. Rich issue context is the cheapest quality lever.</div><div class=\"why\">The one-liner hid scope and expected changes, so the agent planned from almost nothing. A <b>clear problem description</b> repairs the actual input the agent reasons from.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A memory</b> personalizes style; it cannot invent missing scope.</li><li><b>B MCP rate limits</b> throttle doc retrieval; unrelated to PR scope.</li><li><b>D setup-steps resources</b> fix the runner environment, not issue quality.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":9,"topic":3,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image11\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to configure agent1 to support the planned changes.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tDelete line 05 from the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd Use all available tools to the .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd Use all available tools to the instructions in the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the mcp-servers property to the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the agent configuration, replace line 05 with the following.05 tools: [].<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Agent1 profile line 05 pins <b>tools: [read,search,edit]</b>. In agent profiles, omitting the tools key restores the default: everything available. MCP1, meanwhile, is wired through the repository MCP configuration (planned change 3), not the agent profile.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Delete line 05</b>: the restriction itself is what blocks full tool access.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B</b> prose in copilot-instructions.md also leaks into Chat and review (forbidden by change 2), and prose grants no tool access.</li><li><b>C</b> prose inside the profile likewise grants nothing.</li><li><b>D mcp-servers</b> belongs in the repo MCP config per the planned change, not the agent profile.</li><li><b>E tools: []</b> is an empty allow-list: the exact opposite of all-access.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (tools, handoffs)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":10,"topic":3,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image12\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to provide access to the API key of MCP1. The solution must meet the security requirements.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the API key as a GitHub Codespaces user secret scoped to product-api.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the API key as a secret in the Copilot environment of product-api by using a name prefix of COPILOT_MCP_, and then reference the variable name in the mcp.json configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the product-api repository settings, add the API key directly to the .mcp/server.json file by using a plaintext apiKey field.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn product-api, add the API key as a GitHub Actions encrypted secret and reference the secret by using ${{ secrets.KEY }} in the workflow YAML of agent1.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The coding agent cannot see normal Actions secrets, and anything committed to git is visible to every reader. MCP credentials therefore live as <b>COPILOT_MCP_-prefixed secrets in the Copilot environment</b>, referenced by variable name from the MCP config.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>COPILOT_MCP_ secret + mcp.json reference</b>: the only channel the agent MCP runtime reads.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A Codespaces secret</b> serves Codespaces, not the coding agent.</li><li><b>C plaintext apiKey in the repo</b> leaks the key to all readers.</li><li><b>D Actions secret + ${{ secrets }}</b> never reaches the agent MCP runtime.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":11,"topic":3,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image13\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou are evaluating how agent1 will behave after you implement the planned changes.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image14\" src=\"images/image11.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Once MCP1 is configured, agent1 can invoke MCP1 tools autonomously.</td><td class=\"ynv once mcp1 is configured, agent1 can invoke mcp1 tools autonomously. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>agent1 can use the MCP resources and MCP prompts exposed by MCP1.</td><td class=\"ynv agent1 can use the mcp resources and mcp prompts exposed by mcp1. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>If new tools are added to MCP1, they will be available automatically to agent1.</td><td class=\"ynv if new tools are added to mcp1, they will be available automatically to agent1. yes\">Yes</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> An <b>MCP server</b> plugs external capabilities into the agent. MCP defines three surfaces: <b>tools</b> (callable functions), <b>resources</b> (readable data) and <b>prompts</b> (templates). The coding agent consumes configured tools; a remote server re-advertises its tool list every session.</div><div class=\"why\">Configured tools are autonomously callable and refresh dynamically; but MCP1 was configured <b>as a tool</b>, so resources/prompts are not on the table here.</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> MCP1 exposes a <b>create_ticket</b> tool, so agent1 can file tickets by itself. If Litware adds a <b>close_ticket</b> tool next week, it simply shows up. But the agent cannot open a ticket attachment (a resource) or fill in a ticket template (a prompt).</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":12,"topic":3,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image16\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou need to make changes to repo1 to support the planned changes for the agents.<br />\nWhat should you modify?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.github/agents/*.agent.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.vscode/settings.json<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.vscode/mcp.json<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t&lt;project&gt;/.mcp/server.json<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> VS Code Copilot Chat discovers MCP servers from <b>mcp.json</b>. A workspace-level <b>.vscode/mcp.json</b> can be committed to git, so one file configures every developer and every agent centrally. Per-user settings and per-agent profiles cannot do that.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>.vscode/mcp.json</b>: committable, central, repo-wide.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A .agent.md files</b> are per-agent personas, not MCP transport config.</li><li><b>B settings.json</b> is per-user local state: the opposite of central.</li><li><b>D &lt;project&gt;/.mcp/server.json</b> is project-scoped coding-agent config, not repo-wide Chat config.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":13,"topic":3,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository.<br />\nYou use the GitHub Copilot CLI to run an agentic workflow from the terminal.<br />\nDuring execution, the conversation history approaches the context limit. The agent cannot continue the current session unless the amount of retained context is reduced.<br />\nYou need to continue the current session without losing all the prior progress.<br />\nWhich Copilot CLI slash command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/context<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/yolo<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/clear<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/compact<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> LLM CLIs work inside a fixed <b>context window</b>. Long agentic sessions fill it. <b>/compact</b> summarizes the conversation so far into a short brief and keeps going; /context only shows scope, /yolo only controls approvals.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>/compact</b>: same work, smaller context, nothing lost.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A /context</b> views scope; it frees nothing.</li><li><b>B /yolo</b> auto-approves tools; unrelated to context pressure.</li><li><b>C /clear</b> discards history instead of preserving it as a summary.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":14,"topic":3,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository.<br />\nAn agent opens pull requests to the main branch.<br />\nYou need to ensure that changes to .github/workflows/* and /infra/* require approval from designated reviewers before merge.<br />\nWhat should you configure?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta ruleset and an agents.md file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta ruleset and a .copilotignore file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta branch protection rule and a CODEOWNERS file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta branch protection rule and copilot-instructions.md<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Two GitHub mechanisms combine here. A <b>branch protection rule</b> can require pull-request reviews before merge on chosen paths; a <b>CODEOWNERS</b> file names who must review which paths. Together: designated reviewers must approve workflow/infra changes.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Protection rule + CODEOWNERS</b>: gate plus designated approvers.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A agents.md</b> is not an enforcement file.</li><li><b>B .copilotignore</b> only hides files from Copilot; it enforces no approval.</li><li><b>D copilot-instructions.md</b> guides agent behavior but cannot force human approval.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CODEOWNERS</a></li></ul>"},{"n":15,"topic":3,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the following GrtHub Copilot CLI command in a Bash script.<br />\n<img title=\"image17\" src=\"images/image13.png\"><br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image18\" src=\"images/image14.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>The command will prevent runaway loops.</td><td class=\"ynv the command will prevent runaway loops. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>The command will allow the agent to use all tools.</td><td class=\"ynv the command will allow the agent to use all tools. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>The command will allow targeted human intervention at key decision points.</td><td class=\"ynv the command will allow targeted human intervention at key decision points. no\">No</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> This unattended script runs <b>copilot --autopilot --yolo --max-autopilot-continues 10</b>. <b>autopilot</b> = keep working without asking, <b>--yolo</b> = pre-approve every tool, <b>max-autopilot-continues</b> = hard cap on iterations.</div><div class=\"why\">The cap kills runaway loops; yolo unlocks every tool; but fully-automatic plus fully-approved leaves zero points where a human could step in.</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> without the cap, a dependency updater that always finds one more outdated package could loop forever; <b>--max-autopilot-continues 10</b> forces it to stop and report after 10 rounds.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":16,"topic":3,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions for CI.<br />\nYour team is piloting the GitHub Copilot coding agent to autonomously create branches and open pull requests. The repository follows trunk-based development that uses main as the default branch.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent meets the following requirements:<br />\nChanges to main can occur only by using pull requests that have at least one approval.<br />\nWhen a pull request is opened, a validation workflow runs, and the agent can still create branches and open pull requests autonomously.<br />\nHow should you configure the repository? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image20\" src=\"images/image16.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Branch protection (main): <b>Enabled</b><br>Validation trigger: <b>on: pull_request</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Trunk-based development = all work lands on main via short-lived PRs. The agent must be free to branch and open PRs, but main itself only moves through reviewed, CI-validated merges.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Enabled</b> bans direct pushes and demands PR + approval; <b>on: pull_request</b> validates every agent PR before merge.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>Disabled</b> or <b>bypass for direct pushes</b> re-opens unreviewed pushes.</li><li><b>on: push</b> fires on direct pushes and misses PR gating.</li><li><b>on: schedule / workflow_dispatch</b> are not PR-driven, so validation would not gate merges.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li></ul>"},{"n":17,"topic":3,"q":9,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a repository on github.com that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nYou also use the GitHub Copilot CLI locally to reproduce failures and continue the same work from your terminal.<br />\nYou need to verify the current token usage.<br />\nWhich Copilot CLI slash command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/compact<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/diff<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/usage<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/context<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The CLI exposes slash commands for session chores: <b>/usage</b> prints token consumption, /compact shrinks context, /diff shows pending changes, /context shows what the model can see.</div><div class=\"why\">Token usage readout = <b>/usage</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A /compact</b> manages context, <b>B /diff</b> shows changes, <b>D /context</b> shows scope: none reports tokens.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":18,"topic":3,"q":10,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYour team uses a remote GitHub Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for workflows in the software development life cycle (SDLC).<br />\nYou need to commit a workspace-scoped MCP configuration to ensure that GitHub Copilot can connect to the GitHub-hosted MCP endpoint and authenticate by using a GitHub personal access token (PAT).<br />\nHow should you complete the mcp.json configuration file? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image22\" src=\"images/image18.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"type: <b>http</b><br>Authorization: <b>Bearer ${env:GITHUB_PAT}</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> An MCP client config names each server with a transport <b>type</b> (http for a hosted HTTPS endpoint, stdio for a local process) plus auth headers. GitHub personal access tokens authenticate with the <b>Bearer</b> scheme.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>http</b> matches the hosted endpoint; <b>Bearer</b> matches PAT auth.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>stdio / local</b> describe process-local servers, not api.githubcopilot.com.</li><li><b>Token / x-api-key / Digest</b> are not the GitHub PAT scheme.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":19,"topic":3,"q":11,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent named CodeAgent. The .agent.md file of CodeAgent contains the following YAML frontmatter.<br />\n---<br />\nname: CodeAgent<br />\ndescription: Performs repository analysis and code review tasks.<br />\ntools: ['edit', 'execute', 'read', 'search']<br />\n---<br />\nYou need to issue a GitHub Copilot CLI command that preserves execution velocity for read-only tasks by eliminating approval prompts for low-risk tools. The solution must ensure that high-risk tools that can make changes remain available but still require explicit user approval before running.<br />\nWhich command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --deny-tool 'edit,execute'<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-all-tools<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-tool 'read,search'<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-tool 'read,search' --deny-tool 'edit,execute'<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>--allow-tool</b> pre-approves named tools (no more prompts for them); <b>--deny-tool</b> removes tools entirely. Unlisted tools stay usable but keep prompting. Read and search are low-risk; edit and execute are high-risk.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>--allow-tool 'read,search'</b>: fast reads, while edit/execute remain available behind explicit approval.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A deny-only</b> still prompts for reads: no velocity gain.</li><li><b>B --allow-all-tools</b> also pre-approves edit/execute: unsafe.</li><li><b>C plain</b> prompts for everything: slow.</li><li><b>E + --deny</b> deletes edit/execute access, violating remain-available.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":20,"topic":3,"q":12,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions for CI on pull requests.<br />\nYou have a GitHub Copilot coding agent that opens pull requests for backlog items, and your company requires automated checks for agent-generated changes.<br />\nYou plan to standardize success criteria so that pull requests created by agents only succeed when unit tests pass and CodeQL analysis completes.<br />\nYou need to configure a GitHub Actions workflow that runs on pull requests, executes unit tests, and performs CodeQL analysis.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image24\" src=\"images/image20.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Run tests: <b>npm test</b><br>Prepare CodeQL: <b>init</b><br>Run CodeQL: <b>analyze</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> This repo tests with npm, and GitHub code scanning runs CodeQL as a fixed two-step action pair: <b>init</b> prepares the database, <b>analyze</b> runs the queries. upload-sarif exists only for third-party scanners.</div><div class=\"why\">npm runs this repo suite; init then analyze is the only valid CodeQL sequence.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>build</b> compiles instead of testing.</li><li><b>python -m unittest</b> is the wrong ecosystem.</li><li><b>upload-sarif</b> belongs to third-party SARIF uploads, not the native CodeQL flow.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/code-scanning/introduction-to-code-scanning/about-code-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Code scanning / CodeQL</a></li></ul>"},{"n":21,"topic":3,"q":13,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nEngineers assign issues to Copilot, and Copilot creates draft pull requests. The engineers start tasks either by assigning issues on github.com or by using GitHub Copilot Chat in an IDE. Reviewers request updates by leaving pull request comments.<br />\nYou discover that sometimes, Copilot uses outdated requirements after a reviewer posts an updated instruction in a pull request comment, and, in several cases, Copilot fails to resume work from the comment.<br />\nYou need to ensure that iteration requests are applied to the correct pull request session and are processed consistently.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the reviewer has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to mention @copilot in the pull request comments.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the agent has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to mention @github in the task comments.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the agent has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to unassign and reassign the original issue to Copilot.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart a new task from Copilot Chat in the IDE. Instruct the reviewer to mention @github in the task comments.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> After the coding agent opens a PR, reviewers iterate by commenting. For the comment to reach the <b>same agent session</b>, the reviewer must be able to act on the repo (<b>write access</b>) and must ping the agent handle (<b>@copilot</b>) inside the <b>PR comments</b>. Anything else starts or targets the wrong session.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Write access + @copilot in PR comments</b>: routable, same-session iteration.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B</b>: agent-side access is not the gap; @github in task comments targets the wrong place.</li><li><b>C</b> unassign/reassign spawns a new session and drops iteration context.</li><li><b>D</b> a fresh Chat task is disconnected from the PR session.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":22,"topic":3,"q":14,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYour company uses GitHub Copilot custom agents in Microsoft Visual Studio Code.<br />\nThe company also uses the Copilot coding agent on GitHub issues.<br />\nYou have a file named .planner.agent.md that defines an agent named planner.planner has tools set to ['search', 'read', 'fetch']. There are explicit instructions NOT to write or modify any code. The file also defines a handoff labeled Start Implementation to an agent named implementer and sets send to false.<br />\nDevelopers report that after the planner agent produces a plan, implementation sometimes starts immediately in the same conversation, and code changes appear without an explicit agent switch.<br />\nWhen Copilot-created pull requests stall, maintainers review the pull request timeline and session logs. Several stalled sessions show outbound network commands blocked by a firewall, and the repositories do NOT contain a .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image26\" src=\"images/image22.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>The planner config explicitly prevents code changes in the same conversation.</td><td class=\"ynv the planner config explicitly prevents code changes in the same conversation. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>The planner violates its tools via internet access; removing search fixes it.</td><td class=\"ynv the planner violates its tools via internet access; removing search fixes it. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>Repo-level copilot-instructions requiring tests+linters mitigates failing-CI stalls.</td><td class=\"ynv repo-level copilot-instructions requiring tests+linters mitigates failing-ci stalls. yes\">Yes</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The planner allows only search/read/fetch plus written orders never to write code: that <b>is</b> explicit prevention (the observed edits are non-compliance, not config). Web fetching via the allowed <b>fetch</b> tool is legitimate, so network blocks are a firewall problem, while failing CI is a test-discipline problem.</div><div class=\"why\">Config forbids edits (Yes) · fetch is allowed so search-removal fixes nothing (No) · test/linter gates attack failing CI directly (Yes).</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> the planner with only search/read/fetch is like a chef with no stove: it can write the recipe (plan) but physically cannot cook (edit). The handoff button then moves you to the implementer agent, which does have a stove.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":23,"topic":3,"q":15,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository.<br />\nDevelopers use the GitHub Copilot CLI and repository-scoped hooks under .github/hooks/*.json.<br />\nYou need to allow the Copilot CLI to automatically run low-risk Bash commands. The solution must prevent the autonomous execution of high-risk commands, such as sudo, rm -rf /, and curl ... | bash.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a userPromptSubmitted hook that logs prompts and exits nonzero.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd .github/hooks/logs/ to .gitignore.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a sessionStart hook that prints a policy banner.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a preToolUse hook that returns permissionDecision: \"deny\".<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> CLI <b>hooks</b> under .github/hooks/*.json intercept tool use. <b>preToolUse</b> runs before the command and can veto it with permissionDecision deny; userPromptSubmitted only observes prompts and sessionStart only runs once at launch.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>preToolUse + deny</b>: auto-allow the safe, block sudo / rm -rf / / curl-pipe-bash.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A userPromptSubmitted</b> with nonzero exit breaks the session instead of gating one command.</li><li><b>B gitignore</b> hides logs; it enforces nothing.</li><li><b>C sessionStart banner</b> is cosmetic text with no blocking power.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":24,"topic":3,"q":16,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYour company uses Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot Chat.<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses main as the default branch. The repository contains a workspace custom agent stored at .github/agents/release-notes.agent.md.<br />\nA developer switches to a branch named branch1 where the agent file does NOT exist. In the same session, the developer switches to a user profile named profile1.profile1 contains a custom agent file named release-notes.agent.md that has user-invokable set to false.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image28\" src=\"images/image24.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>On main, the agent appears as release-notes.</td><td class=\"ynv on main, the agent appears as release-notes. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>On branch2, the workspace release-notes agent is available.</td><td class=\"ynv on branch2, the workspace release-notes agent is available. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>profile1 agent (user-invokable: false) appears and overrides the workspace agent.</td><td class=\"ynv profile1 agent (user-invokable: false) appears and overrides the workspace agent. no\">No</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> VS Code discovers workspace agents from the <b>checked-out branch</b> (.github/agents/*.agent.md) and profile agents from your user profile. <b>user-invokable: false</b> hides a profile agent from the dropdown entirely.</div><div class=\"why\">Present on main (Yes) · absent on branch2 (No) · hidden profile agent overrides nothing (No).</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> on main the Chat agent picker lists <b>release-notes</b>; check out branch2 (file absent) and it vanishes; a profile agent with user-invokable:false never appears in any picker.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":25,"topic":4,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses a custom GitHub Copilot coding agent defined in the rollout-bot.agent.md file.<br />\nYou need to update a workflow so that agent-profile changes can be rolled back by reverting a single commit and rerunning the workflow. The workflow must check out the exact commit being deployed and apply the agent profile from the repository at that commit.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image30\" src=\"images/image26.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Checkout ref: <b>${{ inputs.ref }}</b><br>Profile path: <b>.github/agents/rollout-bot.md</b><br>Deploy arg: <b>inputs.ref</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Rollback-by-revert works only if the workflow deploys <b>exactly the ref you name</b> (workflow_dispatch input) and reads the agent profile from the repo <b>at that same ref</b>. Workspace agent profiles conventionally live under .github/agents/.</div><div class=\"why\">inputs.ref pins the commit end-to-end; the profile path follows the workspace convention.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>github.ref</b> resolves to the workflow own ref, not your chosen deploy ref.</li><li><b>agents/rollout-bot.md</b> is the wrong directory.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":26,"topic":4,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise organization that has Copilot memory enabled.<br />\nYou create a new repository.<br />\nWhat are two ways that memories will be deleted from the repository? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tmanually by repository members<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen the repository is archived<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen the code that created the memory is deleted<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tautomatically after 28 days<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen pull requests are merged<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A, D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Copilot Memory</b> stores repo facts (conventions, build commands) and personal preferences. Two documented lifecycle rules: owners/admins can <b>manually delete</b> repo facts, and any entry <b>unused for 28 days is auto-deleted</b> (use resets the timer).</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Manual deletion + 28-day expiry</b>: the two documented deletion paths.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B archiving</b> deletes nothing by itself; inactive repos merely let entries expire via the 28-day rule.</li><li><b>C code deletion</b>: stale entries are ignored on validation, not auto-deleted.</li><li><b>E merging PRs</b> never deletes; closed-unmerged PRs can even create memories.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot Memory (concepts)</a></li></ul>"},{"n":27,"topic":4,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub Enterprise organization that uses GitHub Copilot.<br />\nYou discover that GitHub Copilot Chat responses in Microsoft Visual Studio Code are influenced by earlier, unrelated troubleshooting prompts from the same conversation.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the Copilot Chat conversation context is limited to information relevant to the current work item. The solution must minimize effort.<br />\nWhat should you do? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image32\" src=\"images/image28.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Previous prompt irrelevant: <b>Start a new chat.</b><br>Stay focused in IDE: <b>Open related files and close unrelated files.</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Copilot Chat builds context from <b>history + open files</b>. Old troubleshooting turns pollute answers; the cheapest reset is a fresh chat, and curating open files steers every future answer with zero configuration.</div><div class=\"why\">New chat = minimal-effort reset; working-set curation = continuous focus.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Deleting memories destroys useful repo knowledge.</li><li>Deleting one request is fiddly and never fully resets context.</li><li>Agent mode changes behavior instead of scoping context.</li><li>Disabling all features is overkill for a context problem.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":28,"topic":4,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYour company uses GitHub Copilot Enterprise.<br />\nDevelopers use GitHub Copilot agent mode in Microsoft Visual Studio Code on their laptops and Copilot Chat on github.com when they are away from their laptops.<br />\nWhen switching between environments, the developers notice that agent workflows lose continuity because the tools available in Visual Studio Code are unavailable on github.com.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent tools and state are available consistently across environments and can be used from any device without local setup.<br />\nWhat should you do for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image34\" src=\"images/image30.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Cross-device tools, no local setup: <b>GitHub-hosted MCP server</b>.<br>Org-wide tools everywhere: <b>Enable MCP servers in Copilot policy</b>.<div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Local MCP servers (Docker, /mcp add) exist only on one laptop, so github.com sessions lose them. A <b>GitHub-hosted MCP server</b> travels with the account, and the <b>org Copilot policy</b> switch turns servers on for every environment at once.</div><div class=\"why\">Hosted = zero-setup everywhere; policy = one central switch.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Docker/local MCP needs per-machine setup and is absent on github.com.</li><li>/mcp add is per-device manual work.</li><li>Changing mode does not unify tools.</li><li>Disabling toolsets removes tools instead of unifying them.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":29,"topic":4,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues and create draft pull requests.<br />\nYou assign an issue to Copilot. Copilot creates a draft pull request. The pull request timeline shows Copilot started work, followed by status updates. The most recent status update is 55 minutes old.<br />\nYou discover that the agent is no longer making progress.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the work resumes without redoing the completed steps or changing the previously chosen approach.<br />\nWhat should you do? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image36\" src=\"images/image32.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Confirm progress: <b>View session from the pull request</b>.<br>Resume work: <b>post a PR comment mentioning @copilot</b>.<div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A 55-minute-silent agent may just be stuck, not dead. <b>View session</b> shows its live state, and an <b>@copilot PR comment</b> nudges the same session to continue: completed steps and approach survive.</div><div class=\"why\">Observe first, then continue in place: no redo, no approach change.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Repo insights / local git log show code, not agent progress.</li><li>Close/reopen restarts and risks redoing work.</li><li>Marking ready is premature.</li><li>A new PR from Chat starts a new session.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":30,"topic":4,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nYour company restricts GitHub Actions secrets.<br />\nDevelopers need the Copilot coding agent to call an internal dependency-scanning API during its run. The API requires an access token.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the Copilot coding agent can use the token during execution without accessing the repository's Actions secrets and variables. The solution must prevent exposing the token in plaintext.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the token as an Actions repository secret.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the token as a secret in the Copilot environment.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the token in a repository custom instructions file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the token in the agent configuration file.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The coding agent runs outside Actions, so Actions secrets are invisible to it, and anything in git is public to readers. Short-lived credentials for the agent belong in the <b>Copilot environment secrets</b>.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Copilot environment secret</b>: the only secret store the agent runtime reads.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A Actions secret</b> serves workflows, not the agent.</li><li><b>C instructions file / D agent config</b> commit the token in plaintext: a leak.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":31,"topic":5,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a private GitHub repository that has Copilot memory enabled.<br />\nSeveral developers who have write access to the repository make changes across multiple branches, including creating some pull requests that are later closed without merging.<br />\nYour team needs to understand how GitHub Copilot ensures that only task-relevant, up-to-date information influences code suggestions, even when older memories exist.<br />\nHow does Copilot manage memories?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot validates each memory's citations against the current branch before using the memory, and ignores the memory if the referenced code no longer exists.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot stores memories per user, ensuring that only the developer who created a memory can trigger the memory in future sessions.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot stores memories indefinitely until a repository administrator deletes them manually.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot automatically blocks memory creation from pull requests that are closed without merging, to prevent outdated information from being stored.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Memories point at code (<b>citations</b>). Before each use, Copilot re-checks those citations against the current branch: moved or deleted code means the memory is skipped, so stale knowledge can never drive an answer.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Validate-then-ignore-stale</b>: freshness is enforced at use time.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B</b>: repository facts are shared with everyone having memory access, not locked to the creator.</li><li><b>C</b> contradicts the documented 28-day expiry of unused entries.</li><li><b>D</b> is backwards: closed-unmerged PRs can seed memories.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot Memory (concepts)</a></li></ul>"},{"n":32,"topic":5,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image38\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to troubleshoot the issue reported by Ben.<br />\nWhat should you review?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GITHUB_TOKEN permissions block in the agent1 workflow<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe agent session log in the Agents panel<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GitHub Actions runner log for the session job<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GitHub Actions usage metrics of repo1<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> An empty-but-successful PR means the agent did work that produced no diff: only its own narration explains why. The <b>Agents panel session log</b> records reasoning, tool calls and decisions; runner logs show infra output and metrics show aggregates.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Session log</b>: the sole artifact containing agent intent.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A GITHUB_TOKEN perms</b> describe auth scope, not behavior.</li><li><b>C runner log</b> shows infrastructure, not decisions.</li><li><b>D usage metrics</b> aggregate many sessions.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":33,"topic":5,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image39\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou are evaluating the logs of the multi-agent workflow in repo2.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image40\" src=\"images/image34.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>The Github MCP server is enabled for the agent sessions.</td><td class=\"ynv the github mcp server is enabled for the agent sessions. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Workspace isolation is being used by the agent sessions.</td><td class=\"ynv workspace isolation is being used by the agent sessions. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>The logs show exactly two agent sessions started.</td><td class=\"ynv the logs show exactly two agent sessions started. yes\">Yes</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Read the log literally: <b>Passing 1 MCP server(s) [github]</b> means the server reached the session (CLIMCPServerEnabled=false only gates extra CLI-side servers). <b>isolationEnabled: false</b> with no worktree means isolation never engaged. Sessions are the two distinct <b>isNewSession: true</b> IDs.</div><div class=\"why\">Server passed (Yes) · isolation off (No) · two session IDs (Yes).</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example lines:</b> <b>Passing 1 MCP server(s) to SDK: [github]</b> proves statement 1; <b>isolationEnabled: false</b> with no worktree disproves statement 2; two <b>Invoking... isNewSession: true</b> IDs (de04f0fe, 6852c5dc) prove statement 3.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":34,"topic":5,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have the following agent logs.<br />\n<img title=\"image42\" src=\"images/image36.png\"><br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image43\" src=\"images/image37.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>[Statement_2] The agent responded with messages.</td><td class=\"ynv [statement_2] the agent responded with messages. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>[Statement_3] The agent can edit files in the repository.</td><td class=\"ynv [statement_3] the agent can edit files in the repository. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>[Statement_1] The agent analyzed files in the repository.</td><td class=\"ynv [statement_1] the agent analyzed files in the repository. no\">No</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> This log contains only model calls and progress notes. <b>message 0 returned</b> literally means zero messages produced; <b>[panel/editAgent]</b> is a UI panel tag, not a file edit; and no line shows any file read, search or analysis.</div><div class=\"why\">No messages, no edits, no file analysis anywhere: all three No.</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> <b>message 0 returned</b> means the model call ended having produced zero messages: silence, not a reply. <b>[panel/editAgent]</b> names the VS Code side panel, not a file operation: zero evidence of repository edits.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":35,"topic":5,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Copilot code review on pull requests.<br />\nYou plan to add repository-wide code review guidance that will apply to all files.<br />\nYou need Copilot code review to consistently apply the guidance during pull request reviews.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tModify the .github/pull_request_template.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate the instructions in the .github/agents/*.agent.md files.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a .github/instructions/copilot.instructions.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Copilot code review reads repo-wide guidance from <b>.github/copilot-instructions.md</b> (always applied), while <b>.github/instructions/*.instructions.md</b> files are path-scoped via applyTo, agent profiles scope single agents, and the PR template is human boilerplate.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>copilot-instructions.md</b>: the documented repo-wide home for review guidance.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A PR template</b> instructs human authors, not the reviewer.</li><li><b>B .agent.md profiles</b> scope one agent, not all reviews.</li><li><b>C instructions/*.instructions.md</b> applies only to matching paths.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":36,"topic":5,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent and opens draft pull requests for assigned issues.<br />\nEvaluation results show that the agent repeatedly opens pull requests that modify .github/workflows/*.yml to bypass failing checks instead of fixing the underlying code.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent fixes the underlying code instead of bypassing the failing checks.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the GitHub Copilot CLI by using --allow-all.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate .github/copilot-instructions.md.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a branch ruleset.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tDisable GitHub Actions workflows for pull requests created by the agent.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The agent found a loophole: editing workflows silences failing checks. Blocking the loophole stops the symptom; <b>rewriting the repo instructions</b> (never touch workflows, fix the underlying code) cures the strategy.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Update copilot-instructions.md</b>: teach the right goal, not just block the wrong one.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A --allow-all</b> grants even more autonomy: worse.</li><li><b>C ruleset</b> blocks the bypass but the agent stalls instead of fixing code.</li><li><b>D disabling Actions</b> deletes validation entirely.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":37,"topic":5,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You use a GitHub Actions workflow to orchestrate a multi-agent draft-review process for pull requests. The workflow uploads a single combined review-package artifact at the end of each run.<br />\nYou discover that the review-package artifact is occasionally missing from the workflow run history, and you suspect that a user deleted it.<br />\nYou need to identify which user manually deleted a workflow run artifact and when the deletion occurred.<br />\nWhat should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe checks.delete_logs audit log event<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe pull request description and comment history<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe merge commit history<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe artifact.destroy audit log event<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> GitHub records security-relevant actions as named <b>audit log events</b> with actor and timestamp. Artifact deletion is the <b>artifact.destroy</b> event: filter for it to answer who deleted what and when.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>artifact.destroy</b>: the exact event for this question.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A checks.delete_logs</b> belongs to a different event family.</li><li><b>B PR text</b> and <b>C merge history</b> record discussion and commits, never artifact deletion.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},{"n":38,"topic":5,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nCopilot creates a draft pull request for an assigned issue, and the pull request timeline shows Copilot started work.<br />\nAfter 70 minutes, the agent session log stops updating, and the pull request body status stops changing.<br />\nYou need to restart the agent so that it continues the task from the issue context and produces new commits to the existing draft pull request.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tWait for the agent to complete.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tMerge the draft pull request and mention @copilot on the merged pull request.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUnassign the issue from Copilot, and then reassign the issue to Copilot.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSelect Approve and run workflows in the pull request merge box.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A session that stopped (here: the 70-minute mark) will never finish on its own. The supported restart is <b>unassign + reassign the issue</b>: Copilot picks it up fresh from the same issue context. Commenting @copilot is for steering a live PR, not reviving a dead session.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Unassign/reassign</b>: the documented session restart.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A waiting</b> never resolves a stall.</li><li><b>B merging</b> an empty PR is wrong, and merged sessions are closed.</li><li><b>D Approve-and-run</b> gates workflows; it does not revive agents.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":39,"topic":5,"q":9,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses a custom coding agent to run GitHub Actions workflows that create branches, open pull requests, and merge changes after required checks pass.<br />\nYou need to log all agent-initiated actions and ensure that the logs are retained for two years.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure audit log retention.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable log forwarding.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable Git events in the audit log.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure audit log streaming to Azure Event Hubs.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> GitHub keeps audit history briefly; there is no 2-year retention knob. Long-lived compliance history requires <b>streaming audit events out</b> to your own sink (Azure Event Hubs, S3, etc.) as they happen.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Stream to Event Hubs</b>: the only path to multi-year immutable history.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A retention setting</b> with a multi-year value does not exist.</li><li><b>B log forwarding</b> names no compliant sink.</li><li><b>C Git events toggle</b> selects event types, not retention.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},{"n":40,"topic":5,"q":10,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have multiple GitHub Copilot coding agents that run tasks concurrently.<br />\nYou live stream the session log output and see the following.<br />\n<img title=\"image45\" src=\"images/image39.png\"><br />\nWhat is a possible cause of the error?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta postToolUse hook<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta GitHub app installation policy<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta preToolUse hook<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta Copilot firewall policy<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A <b>preToolUse</b> hook inspects each tool call before it runs and can deny it; the denial surfaces right in the live stream. postToolUse only observes completed calls, app policies govern installs, and firewall blocks look like network failures.</div><div class=\"why\">Mid-stream failure during a tool call = hook denial.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A postToolUse</b> runs after success; it cannot break a live call.</li><li><b>B app policy</b> governs installs/permissions, not runtime calls.</li><li><b>D firewall</b> produces network errors, a different shape.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":41,"topic":5,"q":11,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You are analyzing the following agent logs.<br />\n<img title=\"image46\" src=\"images/image40.png\"><br />\nYou need to classify the error in a report for your company's CTO.<br />\nHow should you classify the error?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tContext Issue<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tReasoning error<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tTool misuse<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tNetwork issue<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Classify by where it broke: the agent called the Microsoft Docs MCP search tool and the <b>invocation itself failed before any result</b>. That is <b>tool misuse</b>: not bad reasoning (logic error, call succeeds), not a context problem (prompt/window sizing), not a network outage (transport failure).</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Tool misuse</b>: failed invocation, no result.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A context issue</b> would be sizing/scope of the prompt.</li><li><b>B reasoning error</b> means flawed logic with a working call.</li><li><b>D network issue</b> means the transport failed.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":42,"topic":5,"q":12,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have multiple GitHub Copilot coding agents that run tasks concurrently.<br />\nYou are monitoring the agents from the terminal by using the GitHub CLI.<br />\nAn agent appears stalled.<br />\nYou need to live stream the session log output.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task view and specify the --log and --jq parameters.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task view and specify the --log and --follow parameters.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task list and specify the --web parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task list and specify the --status and --jq parameters.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The <b>gh agent-task</b> extension manages agent tasks. <b>view</b> targets one task, <b>--log</b> prints its session log, <b>--follow</b> keeps streaming like tail -f. --jq only queries static JSON; list only enumerates; --web opens a browser.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>view --log --follow</b>: one task, live log.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A --jq</b> queries a static snapshot: no streaming.</li><li><b>C/D list</b> enumerate tasks; --web opens a browser and --status filters: neither streams a log.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":43,"topic":5,"q":13,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent that has completed a pull request for a security fix in your repository.<br />\nBefore merging, you need to evaluate the quality of the agent's work by using both automated evaluation signals and human review.<br />\nYou review the session log and the pull request.<br />\nWhat are two automated evaluation signals generated by the coding agent's built-in scanning tools? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tlinting errors from the repository's CI pipeline configured in GitHub Actions<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcomments from Copilot code review that suggest improvements to code patterns<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe detection of hardcoded secrets, such as API keys and tokens<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe median time to merge metric reported on the Copilot usage metrics dashboard<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCodeQL findings that identify security vulnerabilities in the generated code<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C, E</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The coding agent ships <b>built-in scanners</b> that judge generated code: <b>secret scanning</b> flags hardcoded keys/tokens, <b>CodeQL code scanning</b> flags vulnerabilities. Your CI lint, AI review prose and usage dashboards are useful but none is a built-in scanner signal.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Secret detection + CodeQL</b>: the two scanner signals.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A repo CI lint</b> is your pipeline, not built-in.</li><li><b>B review comments</b> are reviewer prose, not scanner output.</li><li><b>D time-to-merge</b> measures process, not this code.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/about-secret-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Secret scanning</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/code-scanning/introduction-to-code-scanning/about-code-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Code scanning / CodeQL</a></li></ul>"},{"n":44,"topic":5,"q":14,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository that runs an autonomous agent by using a GitHub Actions workflow. The workflow has the following jobs:<br />\nagent-run that generates trace.json and plan.md<br />\nreview that waits for human approval before continuing<br />\ndeploy that uses the outputs from agent-run<br />\nYou need to make the files inspectable in the GitHub Actions UI and ensure that the files are available to the review and deploy jobs.<br />\nWhat should you do in the workflow?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUse dependency caching to store trace.json and plan.md.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpload trace.json and plan.md as workflow artifacts in agent-run, and download the files inside the jobs.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCommit trace.json and plan.md back to the repository from agent-run.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore trace.json and plan.md on a network share and have later jobs retrieve them from the share.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Runners are ephemeral and jobs isolated: files pass between jobs only through <b>upload-artifact / download-artifact</b>. Caches are best-effort (for dependencies), commits pollute history, and network shares add infra runners cannot rely on.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Upload in agent-run, download in review/deploy</b>: the native handoff.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A caching</b> is best-effort dependency storage, not a file handoff.</li><li><b>C committing</b> generated files pollutes the repo.</li><li><b>D network share</b> is external infra with no availability guarantee.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/storing-workflow-data-as-artifacts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow artifacts</a></li></ul>"},{"n":45,"topic":5,"q":15,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  A developer uses the GitHub Copilot CLI in plan mode.<br />\nCopilot produces a plan.<br />\nWhat does Copilot do next?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tsaves the plan to plan.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcreates a branch named /copilot/plan in the repository<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\topens a pull request that has the plan as a comment<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tbegins the implementation<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Plan mode</b> exists so nothing happens except planning: Copilot investigates and writes the plan to <b>plan.md</b>, then stops. Branches, PRs and implementation are all actions beyond planning.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>saves the plan to plan.md</b>: planning-only, by definition.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B branch / C PR / D implement</b> each contradict plan-mode semantics.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":46,"topic":6,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image47\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to resolve the issue of the agents generating conflicting output. The solution must meet the implementation guidelines.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd shared/config.yaml to a CODEOWNERS file that requires SG_Review approval before any changes can be merged.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure each agent to work on a separate branch and add a required status check that detects file-level overlap before either pull request can be merged.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure tools: ['read', 'search'] in both agent profiles to prevent either agent from writing files.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a concurrency group on both agent workflows so that only one workflow runs at a time.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Three constraints must hold at once: agents run <b>concurrently</b>, on <b>isolated branches</b>, with <b>file conflicts caught before merge</b>. Only separate branches plus a required overlap-detecting status check satisfies all three.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Separate branches + overlap check</b>: parallel, concurrent, safe.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A CODEOWNERS</b> adds review but no branch isolation: outputs still collide.</li><li><b>C read-only tools</b> makes the agents unable to do their jobs.</li><li><b>D concurrency group</b> serializes the workflows, violating concurrency.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li></ul>"},{"n":47,"topic":6,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that contains the following custom agent files:<br />\nA file named planner.agent.md that includes YAML frontmatter with a handoffs entry that has label: Start Implementation, agent: implementer, and prompt: Now implement the plan outlined above<br />\nA file named implementer.agent.md that is in the same directory as planner.agent.md and includes YAML frontmatter that has name: IMPLEMENTER<br />\nYou add a third agent file named review.agent.md.review.agent.md includes YAML frontmatter that has name: code-review.<br />\nYou make the following changes to planner.agent.md:<br />\nUpdate the existing handoff to include send: true and model: GPT-5.2 (copilot).<br />\nAdd a second handoff that has label: Run Review, agent: code-review, and prompt: Review the code changes made in the previous step.<br />\nNo other agent files are modified.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image48\" src=\"images/image41.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>The Run Review handoff resolves to review.agent.md.</td><td class=\"ynv the run review handoff resolves to review.agent.md. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>The Start Implementation handoff fails to identify the target agent.</td><td class=\"ynv the start implementation handoff fails to identify the target agent. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>Selecting Start Implementation switches chat + auto-submits via GPT-5.2 (copilot).</td><td class=\"ynv selecting start implementation switches chat + auto-submits via gpt-5.2 (copilot). yes\">Yes</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Per VS Code docs, <b>handoffs.agent is the target agent identifier = the file-name stem</b> (planner example uses agent: implementation). <b>send: true</b> auto-submits the prompt; <b>model</b> takes the qualified Model (vendor) form.</div><div class=\"why\">code-review matches no file stem (No) · implementer resolves (No to fails) · send:true + GPT-5.2 (copilot) auto-submits (Yes).</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> planner.agent.md has identifier <b>planner</b>. Handoff <b>agent: implementer</b> looks for implementer.agent.md (found, resolves). Handoff <b>agent: code-review</b> looks for code-review.agent.md (missing: review.agent.md does not count, identifiers match file stems, not frontmatter names).</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (incl. handoffs)</a></li></ul>"},{"n":48,"topic":6,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions to validate pull requests opened by the GitHub Copilot coding agent. The workflow runs unit tests and a linter on pull request triggers, and Copilot opens draft pull requests on dedicated branches while iterating by using commits.<br />\nYou discover that when multiple Copilot sessions push updates to the same pull request branch in quick succession, multiple workflow runs execute concurrently.<br />\nYou need to enable parallel workflow executions across different pull request branches.<br />\nHow should you configure workflow-level concurrency? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image50\" src=\"images/image43.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Scope: <b>Set concurrency at the workflow level.</b><br>Expression: <b>group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A <b>concurrency group</b> serializes runs sharing a key. Keyed per branch (<b>github.ref</b>), rapid pushes to one PR queue while different PRs run in parallel. The key must scope the <b>workflow</b>, and any key containing <b>run_id</b> (unique per run) groups nothing.</div><div class=\"why\">Workflow-level per-branch group: parallel across PRs, serialized per branch.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>Job-level only</b> scopes the wrong level: pushes still overlap.</li><li><b>strategy.matrix</b> limits parallelism; it never groups runs.</li><li><b>head_ref || run_id / run_id</b>: run_id is unique, so every run is its own group.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-jobs/using-concurrency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow concurrency groups</a></li></ul>"},{"n":49,"topic":6,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that contains an agent named Orchestrator.Orchestrator delegates work to the following specialized subagents:<br />\nPlanner reviews issues and creates a plan of action.<br />\nImplementer writes code based on the plan of action.<br />\nReviewer reviews the code.<br />\nYou create a new agent named Summarizer that produces a concise summary of the work performed by the other agents.<br />\nYou need to ensure that Orchestrator can invoke Summarizer as part of its workflow.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Reviewer agent, add a handoffs entry that points to the Summarizer agent.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Reviewer agent, add Summarizer to the agents list.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Orchestrator agent, add Summarizer to the tools list.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Orchestrator agent, add Summarizer to the agents list.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> An <b>Orchestrator</b> pattern delegates phases to subagents. Model-driven delegation reads the <b>agents array in the Orchestrator frontmatter</b>: only listed agents are callable. Handoffs drive chat buttons; the tools list holds tools, not agents.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Add Summarizer to the Orchestrator agents list</b>: listing is what makes it callable.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A/B Reviewer</b> is not the invoking agent.</li><li><b>C tools list</b>: subagents are agents, not tools.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":50,"topic":6,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a multi-agent GitHub Actions workflow that uploads review artifacts for each run.<br />\nYou discover that some workflow run artifacts are being deleted manually.<br />\nYou need to use your organization's audit log data to identify which user deleted the artifacts.<br />\nWhich audit log search filter should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\trepo:&lt;org&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\taction:workflows.run<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\toperation:remove<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\taction:artifact.destroy<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The audit log is queried by field filters. Event-type filtering uses the <b>action:</b> qualifier, and artifact deletion is the <b>artifact.destroy</b> event.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>action:artifact.destroy</b>: exactly the deletion events.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A repo:</b> filters by repository, not by event.</li><li><b>B action:workflows.run</b> matches run events, not deletions.</li><li><b>C operation:remove</b> is not a valid audit filter.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},{"n":51,"topic":6,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent named Orchestrator that runs a multi-phase workflow by using the following subagents:<br />\nExplorer gathers context by using read-only tools.<br />\nModifier applies focused edits.<br />\nYou are adding a new agent named Summarizer that generates a concise summary after modifications are complete. Summarizer includes the following YAML frontmatter.<br />\n<img title=\"image52\" src=\"images/image45.png\"><br />\nThe Orchestrator agent lists all three agents in its agents property.<br />\nAfter adding the Summarizer agent, Orchestrator successfully runs Explorer and Modifier but fails to run Summarizer.<br />\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSummarizer cannot be invoked as a subagent because disable-model-invocation is set to true.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tOrchestrator is missing a handoff entry to trigger Summarizer.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tOrchestrator cannot call Summarizer because user-invocable is set to false.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSummarizer is missing the editing tools required to complete the workflow.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Three frontmatter flags, three meanings: <b>agents[]</b> = callable subagents, <b>user-invocable: false</b> = humans cannot @-mention it, <b>disable-model-invocation: true</b> = the model cannot call it. Explorer/Modifier run through the same agents array, so the array works: only Summarizer self-blocks.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>disable-model-invocation: true</b>: the one flag that bars model-driven calls.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B handoffs</b> drive chat buttons, not agents-array calls: Explorer/Modifier prove handoffs unnecessary.</li><li><b>C user-invocable</b> gates humans, not the model.</li><li><b>D editing tools</b>: a summary needs only fetch.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":52,"topic":6,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that runs an agentic software development lifecycle (SDLC) workflow by using GitHub Actions. The workflow uses the following three executors implemented as scripts: spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer, and plan_merger.<br />\nYou need to coordinate multiple specialized agents so that analysis and risk review run in parallel, and then a final executor merges the outputs into a single plan. The orchestration pattern must fan out one request to multiple executors, and then fan in the results to a final executor.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow definition? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image53\" src=\"images/image46.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Fan-out/fan-in: <b>needs: [spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer]</b> on plan_merger<br>Concurrency at <b>workflow level</b>, group <b>multiagent-${{ github.ref }}</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>needs</b> builds the DAG: plan_merger waits until both parallel analyzers finish (fan-out then fan-in). <b>Workflow-level concurrency</b> keyed per ref keeps branches parallel while same-branch pushes serialize.</div><div class=\"why\">needs = wait for both; per-ref workflow group = parallel branches, serial pushes.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-jobs/using-concurrency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow concurrency groups</a></li></ul>"},{"n":53,"topic":7,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Copilot Chat in Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Custom agents are stored in the repository under version control.<br />\nYour team uses a multi-agent workflow where a planner agent produces an implementation plan that is then handed off to an implementation agent to make changes.<br />\nRecent prompts cause the planner agent to start editing files and running commands before the plan is approved.<br />\nYou need to configure the planner agent to meet the following requirements:<br />\nUse only read-only tools.<br />\nHand off to the implementation agent only after the plan is approved.<br />\nHow should you configure the agent? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image55\" src=\"images/image48.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"tools: <b>[search,read,fetch]</b><br>handoff send: <b>false</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Read-only means <b>no write power at all</b>: search, read and fetch only qualify, while edit/commit/push/merge/publish all write. And <b>send: false</b> (the default) keeps the handoff a button the human fires after approving the plan.</div><div class=\"why\">Read-only toolset + human-gated handoff.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Any list containing edit/commit/push/merge/publish grants write power.</li><li>send:true would auto-submit the handoff before approval.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (incl. handoffs)</a></li></ul>"},{"n":54,"topic":7,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image57\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to implement the security requirements for agent1.<br />\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.<br />\n<img title=\"image58\" src=\"images/image50.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"1. <b>Add the plan job first</b> (uploads plan output as artifact)<br>2. <b>Create the plan-review environment with SG_Review as required reviewers</b><br>3. <b>Add the execute job with needs: [plan] and environment: plan-review</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The security demands: <b>only SG_Review approves plan outputs</b>, execution waits for that approval. GitHub <b>environments</b> gate jobs behind named required reviewers; <b>needs</b> orders the jobs. Order is load-bearing: produce, gate, then execute.</div><div class=\"why\">Produce the plan, gate it behind SG_Review, then execute the approved plan.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Auto-advance on green skips the required approval.</li><li>A 2-approval main-branch ruleset gates pushes, not plan outputs.</li><li>SG_Dev as reviewers violates SG_Review-only approval.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/managing-workflow-runs-and-deployments/managing-deployments/managing-environments-for-deployment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Environments + required reviewers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":55,"topic":7,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image60\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to resolve the scoping issue associated to agent1.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tTo the profile of agent1, add a custom instruction specifying that the agent must NOT access billing-service or infra-terraform.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd a permissions block to the agent1 workflow in product-api.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a fine-grained personal access token (PAT) scoped to product-api and store the PAT as a GitHub Actions secret for agent1 to use.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a ruleset for billing-service and infra-terraform that blocks push access from the github-actions bot account.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The agent touched repos outside its scope because its <b>credential</b> allowed it. A <b>fine-grained PAT</b> can be limited to one repository: scoped to product-api, billing-service and infra-terraform become unreachable. Prose instructions are unenforceable, and rulesets only block pushes.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Scoped PAT</b>: fix the scope at the credential layer.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A prose instruction</b> (do not access X) is unenforced: agents can ignore it.</li><li><b>B permissions block</b> scopes GITHUB_TOKEN, not the agent session credential.</li><li><b>D bot-push ruleset</b> blocks only pushes by one identity: reads continue, and the agent may not even push as the bot.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fine-grained personal access tokens</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-guides/automatic-token-authentication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GITHUB_TOKEN permissions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":56,"topic":7,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image61\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nWhile upgrading App1, the agent identifies 47 issues, including a security vulnerability, and 46 API incompatibilities across different projects.<br />\nWhich two actions are unsafe to delegate to the agent and require human involvement? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tValidate the assessment.md file for accuracy.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tGenerate the assessment.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tApprove all Git commits.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tReview the plan.md file for dependencies.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tValidate whether the tasks.md file exists.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A, C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The agent surfaced 47 issues including a <b>security vulnerability</b>. Generating reports and analyzing dependencies is exactly agent work; but <b>judging correctness of security-relevant findings</b> and <b>approving commits</b> (the merge gate) require accountable human judgment.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Validate assessment + approve commits</b>: the two human gates.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B generating</b> the assessment is core agent work.</li><li><b>D reviewing plan dependencies</b> is delegable analysis.</li><li><b>E checking tasks.md exists</b> is a trivial filesystem check.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-app-modernization/concepts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App modernization: assessment, plan, tasks.md</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-upgrade/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot upgrade overview</a></li></ul>"},{"n":57,"topic":7,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot CLI to run autonomous tasks.<br />\nYou need to validate each generated command before it runs. Any commands that attempt to modify paths outside the repository must be blocked.<br />\nHow should you complete the YAML? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image62\" src=\"images/image52.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"event: <b>pre-exec</b><br>config: <b>.copilot/hooks.yaml</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> This hook format (version/hooks/cli) guards generated shell commands: the script reads <b></b> and exits nonzero to veto. Vetoing must happen <b>before execution</b>, and the CLI harness reads <b>.copilot/hooks.yaml</b>, never an Actions workflow path.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>pre-exec + .copilot/hooks.yaml</b>: validate before run, in the file the harness reads.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>post-exec</b> runs after the command: blocking there is too late.</li><li><b>.github/workflows/hooks.yaml</b> is a CI workflow path the CLI harness never reads.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":58,"topic":7,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses GitHub Actions for CI/CD.<br />\nYou plan to enable an agent to update and deploy workflows that access production environment secrets.<br />\nYou need to select an autonomy level for a compliance-sensitive workflow.<br />\nWhich autonomy level should you select?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tread-only analysis with no ability to trigger workflows<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tautonomous execution limited to non-production environments<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\thuman-in-the-loop with required manual approvals for production<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tfully autonomous execution without approvals<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Autonomy levels</b> trade speed for control. Compliance-sensitive production deploys with secrets demand a human release gate: the agent may prepare everything, but only <b>required manual approvals</b> let it touch production.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Human-in-the-loop + manual production approvals</b>: preparation automated, release human.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A read-only</b> cannot deploy at all.</li><li><b>B non-prod-only</b> excludes the required production target.</li><li><b>D fully autonomous</b> with secrets and production access violates compliance.</li></ul><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"}];
